Short answer: build the one pooled model now and start with 
  pick-a-ticker, but plan for cross-sectional ranking as the real payoff.
  They aren't actually a fork in training — both consume the same
  pooled_xgb.joblib. The only difference is what you do with the scores at
  inference. So you don't have to commit the model to either; you commit  the consumption layer, and you can add the second one later without
  retraining.
  Here's the reasoning, because the distinction matters more than it looks:
  Pick-a-ticker uses an absolute threshold — "is P(up) ≥ 0.65?" That
  demands the probability be well-calibrated in absolute terms, which is
  the single hardest thing to get right and is exactly where single-name
  models fall apart. A model can be genuinely useful and still rarely cross  0.65, so you'd see lots of NO_TRADE.

  Cross-sectional ranking only needs the scores to be correctly ordered
  across stocks on the same day — "rank all 90 today, go long the top few,
  short the bottom few." That's a strictly weaker, more achievable
  requirement than absolute calibration, and it's the standard quant use of  a pooled model precisely because relative ranking survives where
  absolute probability doesn't. It also gets you a more honest evaluation:
  "did the top basket beat the bottom basket?" is a money question, unlike
  AUC.

  So if the actual goal is making money on single names, ranking is the
  better-suited design — that's where a pooled model's edge actually lives.
  My recommendation, in order:

  1. Now: one pooled model + pooled walk-forward AUC + the holdout check.
  This just answers "did pooling create signal at all?" Don't build either
  consumption layer until that number moves off ~0.49 — if it doesn't,
  neither inference style will save it.
  2. Keep pick-a-ticker as the default inference for the existing API/CLI,
  because it's already your product surface and it's a near-zero change
  (load the pooled model instead of the per-ticker one).
  3. Add cross-sectional ranking as a second, thin layer on top of the same  model once the pooled AUC justifies it — a "rank the universe today,
  here are the top longs/shorts" view. That's where you'd actually expect
  an edge, but it's also a bigger product change (you're now outputting a
  basket, not a per-ticker signal), so it shouldn't gate the first
  measurement.

  In other words: the model file is the same either way, so build that
  first and let the measured AUC decide whether ranking is even worth
  wiring up. Want me to proceed on that basis — collapse to one pooled
  path, one saved model, pick-a-ticker inference — and leave a clear seam
  where ranking can bolt on later?